{
  "meta": {
    "site": "https://github.com/zengfr/n8n-workflow-all-templates",
    "tokenfree": "https://tokenyep.com/",
    "name": "Send, chase, and e-signature with Airtable, TurboDocx, Drive and Slack",
    "wechat": "youandme10086",
    "id": 18209,
    "update_time": "2026-09-27"
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "e655ee84-200e-4003-91a0-dbd73339533a",
      "name": "Sticky Note 0",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1328,
        1008
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 900,
        "height": 1244,
        "content": "# Send agreements from Airtable for signature and file them in Drive\n\nWatches a Google Drive folder, reads the matching contract rows from Airtable, generates a\npersonalised agreement per row with TurboDocx, sends each one for e-signature, chases anyone\nwho has not signed after 3 days, and — once signed — files the executed PDF and its audit\ntrail back into Drive, updates Airtable, posts to Slack and emails accounting.\n\n**Every customer-visible step posts to Slack**, so the channel is a live feed of the\ncontract pipeline.\n\n## The three lanes\n\n| Lane | Trigger | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A - Dispatch** | A file lands in the watched Drive folder | Generate and send every Airtable row marked `Ready to send` |\n| **B - Chase** | Schedule, daily | Remind anyone unsigned for 3+ days, once |\n| **C - Settle** | TurboDocx webhook | Archive, record, announce, bill |\n\nLanes B and C run on their own triggers. You do not wire them to lane A.\n\nOne credential does all of it: TurboDocx generates the document, sends it for signature,\nchases it and returns the signed copy with its audit trail. A signature-only tool cannot\nvary the document body per row. The free tier covers 5 signatures a month, so you can run\nthis end to end before paying for anything - https://www.turbodocx.com\n\n## Setup\n\n1. **TurboDocx** - create an API key (Settings > API Keys) and add the TurboDocx credential.\n   Docs: https://docs.turbodocx.com\n2. **Template** - upload a .docx to TurboDocx containing your merge variables *and* your\n   signature anchors, then put its ID in `YOUR_TURBODOCX_TEMPLATE_ID`. Read the\n   *Anchors vs merge variables* note before you author it - this is the step people get wrong.\n3. **Airtable** - create a table with the columns listed in the Airtable note, then fill in\n   `YOUR_AIRTABLE_BASE_ID` and `YOUR_AIRTABLE_TABLE_ID`.\n4. **Google Drive** - set the watched folder and the archive folder IDs.\n5. **Slack** - set `YOUR_SLACK_CHANNEL_ID` on all seven Slack nodes.\n6. **Gmail** - set the accounting distribution address on the final node.\n7. Activate the workflow. Lane C's webhook registers itself with TurboDocx on activation."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ae06239b-197e-4e8e-8c6e-5ae25561dda1",
      "name": "Sticky Note 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1328,
        2272
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 900,
        "height": 640,
        "content": "## Anchors vs merge variables - read this before authoring your template\n\nBoth are single-brace tokens. They differ **only in the value you pass at generation time.**\n\n**Merge variables** - `{clientName}`, `{company}`, `{amount}`, `{startDate}`.\nPass the real value. TurboDocx substitutes it.\n\n**Signature anchors** - `{client_sig}`, `{client_date}`.\nPass **the token as its own value**: variable `client_sig` gets the literal text\n`{client_sig}`. The tag is substituted with itself, so it survives into the generated\ndocument, and TurboSign then finds it by text search when it places the signature field.\nLook at the *Generate agreement* node - the last two variables do exactly this.\n\n**Never leave a brace token without a value.** It renders as the literal word `undefined`\nin your contract.\n\nUse `\"placement\": \"replace\"` on each field. It is the only placement that covers the\nanchor text - the others leave the raw token visible in the signed PDF.\n\nAnchor matching is case-insensitive, so `{Sig}` and `{sig}` are the same anchor. Every\noccurrence of an anchor gets its own field, so keep them unique."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "583faf2a-66ec-4530-a3ac-703d5819815b",
      "name": "Sticky Note 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1328,
        2928
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 900,
        "height": 760,
        "content": "## Airtable table\n\n| Column | Type |\n|---|---|\n| `Client Name` | Single line text |\n| `Company` | Single line text |\n| `Email` | Email |\n| `Amount` | Number |\n| `Start Date` | Date |\n| `Status` | Single select: `Ready to send`, `Generated`, `Sent for signature`, `Reminded`, `Signed`, `Declined`, `Failed` |\n| `Deliverable ID` | Single line text |\n| `Document ID` | Single line text |\n| `Sent At` | Date and time |\n| `Reminded At` | Date and time |\n| `Signed At` | Date and time |\n| `Signed Doc URL` | URL |\n\n`Document ID` is the join key. Lane C receives a `document_id` from the webhook and looks\nthe row back up by it, so nothing downstream needs to remember which run sent what."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "9a55ac35-b44e-472e-a29a-ad53d5a7728f",
      "name": "Sticky Note 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        2352,
        1008
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 5,
        "width": 1180,
        "height": 300,
        "content": "## Lane A - Dispatch\n\nA file landing in the watched Drive folder is the go signal. The workflow then reads every\nAirtable row marked `Ready to send` and processes them one at a time.\n\nGeneration and sending both use `onError: continueErrorOutput`, so one bad row (a missing\nemail, a deleted template) marks itself `Failed` and shouts in Slack instead of killing the\nwhole batch."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
